// @check
// @error-at 19:19 union members 'EntityId' and 'i32' have the same runtime representation, so `is` cannot tell them apart — a nominal newtype is erased to its base at run time
// A newtype BESIDE ITS OWN BASE in a union is not a type this language can represent, so
// the spelling is refused rather than answered wrongly at `is`.
//
// A union box carries ONE tag and `emitIs` answers `x is T` by comparing it. A brand is
// minted as a second arena index over the same value and `canonEmitTypeNames` erases it
// before codegen (`newtype-design.md` §3), so `EntityId` and `i32` reach that tag
// identically: without this reject BOTH `x is EntityId` and `x is i32` answer TRUE, for a
// value built through either arm.
//
// #1340's membership test cannot rescue it, and the difference is the point: a
// literal-union member is a runtime-observable SUBSET of its base, so `x is K` re-lowers
// to a payload compare; a brand has no runtime witness at all.
//
// The INVERTED CONTROL is `newtype-union-distinct-reps.vl` — the same union shape with
// arms whose reps DIFFER (`EntityId | string`) runs and narrows correctly, which is what
// makes this reject attributable to the rep collision and not to a newtype in a union.
type EntityId = new i32
const u: EntityId | i32 = 5 as EntityId
if u is EntityId { print("id") } else { print("raw") }
